(3) The classification shall be documented in the engine design data package and shall be reviewed by the certification authority.

CS-E 34.15-1: The engine must be designed to prevent the occurrence of a hazardous failure condition resulting from the incorrect assembly of components. The design must ensure that components which, if assembled in an incorrect configuration, could lead to a hazardous failure condition, are either physically incompatible or are protected by a positive means of identification. This requirement applies to all components where incorrect assembly could result in:

(a) Loss of thrust or power;
(b) Uncontrolled release of hazardous energy;
(c) Structural failure; or
(d) Fire or explosion.

The means of identification shall be such that it is not easily removed or obscured during normal maintenance operations. Where physical incompatibility is not feasible, the design shall incorporate a positive locking mechanism or a unique keying feature that prevents the installation of the component in an incorrect orientation or position. The effectiveness of these provisions shall be demonstrated by analysis, test, or both, in accordance with the applicable means of compliance. The engine manufacturer shall provide maintenance instructions that clearly identify the correct assembly configuration and the consequences of incorrect assembly.

The applicant shall demonstrate that the lightning protection system of the engine, including its High-Intensity Radio Frequency (HIRF) protection, is effective when installed on the aircraft. Where full-scale lightning trials are not conducted on a complete, true-to-type powerplant, the applicant shall provide evidence that the lightning protection system of the engine is compatible with the aircraft lightning protection system. This demonstration may be achieved through a combination of the following means:

1.  Analysis of the lightning current distribution and potential differences between engine components and the airframe, considering the specific installation configuration;
2.  Test data from component-level lightning trials that simulate the current injection points and magnitudes expected during an aircraft strike;
3.  Verification that the engine’s lightning protection system does not create hazardous potential differences or arcing that could compromise the integrity of the engine or the aircraft structure; and
4.  Confirmation that the engine’s lightning protection system is designed to handle the maximum expected lightning current without failure, as defined in the applicable lightning protection standards.

The applicant shall document the rationale for any deviations from full-scale testing and provide a detailed analysis showing that the proposed methods adequately address the risks associated with lightning strikes to the engine installation.

CS-E 11.15.1: The power and torque of a reciprocating engine shall be corrected to the standard atmosphere. When the measured power or torque varies with the temperature of the combustion chamber or the heat-transfer fluid, the measured values shall be corrected to the standard atmosphere using the following formula:

P_corr = P_meas × (1 + 0.0015 (T - T_std))

Where:
P_corr is the corrected power in kilowatts (kW);
P_meas is the measured power in kilowatts (kW);
T is the measured temperature of the combustion chamber or heat-transfer fluid in degrees Celsius (°C);
T_std is the standard temperature of the combustion chamber or heat-transfer fluid in degrees Celsius (°C).

AMC CS-E 11.15.1: The standard temperature T_std shall be determined based on the engine manufacturer’s data or, in the absence of such data, shall be assumed to be 15 °C for air-cooled engines and 100 °C for liquid-cooled engines. The correction factor shall be applied to all measured power and torque values to ensure consistency with the standard atmosphere conditions defined in CS-E 11.1.

AMC CS-E.10.10(b)

The following trials may be conducted on a different unit of identical design, provided that the unit is of the same production series and has been subjected to the same manufacturing processes and quality control procedures as the unit undergoing the primary qualification:

1.  The endurance run, provided that the unit has previously completed a successful run-in period and has demonstrated stable performance parameters;
2.  The cold start trials, provided that the ambient temperature conditions are within the specified limits for the engine type;
3.  The vibration analysis, provided that the mounting configuration and support structure are identical to those used for the primary qualification unit;
4.  The oil system leak check, provided that the oil pressure and flow rates are within the specified tolerances;
5.  The fuel system leak check, provided that the fuel pressure and flow rates are within the specified tolerances.

The applicant must provide evidence that the alternative unit is representative of the production population and that the results obtained are applicable to the engine type being certified.

AMC CS-E.10.10: Output reference charts at zero altitude shall be established for each powerplant configuration. These charts must be drawn up to demonstrate compliance with the specified output requirements at the following points:

(a) The maximum continuous power rating, if applicable;
(b) The maximum take-off power rating;
(c) The maximum continuous power rating for the specific operating condition; and
(d) Any other power rating specified in the type design data.

The charts shall plot indicated power or shaft power against engine speed, covering the full range of engine speeds from the minimum idle speed to the maximum rated speed. For each point, the corresponding manifold pressure, fuel flow, and oil pressure shall be indicated. The data shall be corrected to standard atmosphere conditions at zero altitude. Where the powerplant is equipped with a supercharger or turbocharger, the charts shall also include the pressure ratio and intercooler outlet temperature. The calibration testing shall be conducted in accordance with CS-E.10.10, ensuring that the measured values are within the tolerances specified in the type design data.

AMC CS-E.10.1: To adjust the measured shaft rotational speed to the reference day conditions, the following equation shall be used:

$$N_{ref} = N_{meas} \sqrt{\frac{T_{ref}}{T_{meas}}}$$

Where:
$N_{ref}$ is the shaft rotational speed at reference day conditions (rpm);
$N_{meas}$ is the measured shaft rotational speed (rpm);
$T_{ref}$ is the reference day ambient temperature (K);
$T_{meas}$ is the measured ambient temperature (K).

This adjustment assumes that the shaft rotational speed is proportional to the square root of the absolute ambient temperature, consistent with the ideal gas law and the assumption of constant volumetric flow rate through the engine. The reference day conditions are defined as 15 °C (288.15 K) and 101.325 kPa. The measured ambient temperature shall be corrected for any non-standard pressure effects if the deviation from the standard atmosphere exceeds 10 kPa, in accordance with the engine manufacturer's approved test procedures.

AMC CS-E.10.10

The measured speed of the turbine, $N_{meas}$, shall be corrected to the equivalent speed at standard day conditions, $N_{std}$, to facilitate comparison with the certified performance data. The correction shall account for the ambient temperature and pressure variations from the standard atmosphere defined in CS-E.10. The equivalent speed at standard day is calculated using the following relationship:

$$N_{std} = N_{meas} \sqrt{\frac{T_{amb}}{T_{std}}}$$

Where:
$N_{meas}$ is the measured rotational speed in revolutions per minute (r.p.m.);
$N_{std}$ is the corrected rotational speed at standard day in revolutions per minute (r.p.m.);
$T_{amb}$ is the ambient absolute temperature in Kelvin (K);
$T_{std}$ is the standard day absolute temperature, which is 288.15 K.

This correction assumes that the turbine speed is proportional to the square root of the absolute temperature, consistent with the ideal gas law and the definition of equivalent power. The applicant shall document the specific ambient conditions under which the test was conducted and apply this correction to all reported speed parameters.

The failure analysis shall identify all components and control systems whose failure could result in a hazardous or catastrophic failure condition. This analysis shall include, but not be limited to, the following:

(a) Engine structure, including the crankcase, cylinder block, and head;
(b) Crankshaft, connecting rods, and pistons;
(c) Valves, valve springs, and valve gear;
(d) Fuel system components, including fuel pumps, injectors, and fuel lines;
(e) Ignition system components, including spark plugs, ignition coils, and distributor components;
(f) Oil system components, including oil pumps, oil lines, and oil filters;
(g) Cooling system components, including radiators, water pumps, and thermostats;
(h) Engine control systems, including electronic control units (ECU), sensors, and actuators;
(i) Propeller control systems, if applicable;
(j) Starting system components;
(k) Exhaust system components;
(l) Any other component or system whose failure could affect engine performance or safety.

The analysis shall consider both single and multiple failures, including common cause failures, and shall evaluate the consequences of each failure on engine operation and aircraft safety.

The failure analysis shall consider the following combinations of failures:

(a) A single failure of any component, including but not limited to a cylinder, piston, connecting rod, or crankshaft journal;

(b) A single failure of any component, followed by a second failure of a different component, where the second failure is a direct consequence of the first failure;

(c) A single failure of any component, followed by a second failure of a different component, where the second failure is not a direct consequence of the first failure, but is a credible failure mode that could occur independently;

(d) A single failure of any component, followed by a second failure of a different component, where the second failure is a direct consequence of the first failure, and the combined effect of the two failures results in a hazardous condition.

The analysis shall demonstrate that the engine can continue to operate safely, or that the consequences of the failure are acceptable, in accordance with the applicable airworthiness requirements.

The failure analysis shall consider the following combinations of failures:

(a) A single failure of any component, system, or part of the engine, including the failure of any single actuator, sensor, or control element;

(b) A single failure of any component, system, or part of the engine, followed by a second failure of any other component, system, or part of the engine, provided that the second failure is not a consequence of the first failure;

(c) A single failure of any component, system, or part of the engine, followed by a second failure of any other component, system, or part of the engine, where the second failure is a consequence of the first failure, if such a sequence is credible;

(d) A single failure of any component, system, or part of the engine, followed by a second failure of any other component, system, or part of the engine, where the second failure is a consequence of the first failure, and the first failure is a single failure of a component, system, or part of the engine that is not a single failure of a component, system, or part of the engine that is a single failure of a component, system, or part of the engine.

The vibration tests shall be conducted over the entire operating speed range of the engine, from the minimum idle speed to the maximum continuous operating speed. For each test point, the engine shall be operated at a constant speed for a duration sufficient to ensure that the vibration levels have reached a steady state. The test matrix shall include, at a minimum, the following specific speed points:

(a) Minimum idle speed;
(b) Maximum idle speed;
(c) The speed corresponding to the maximum torque output;
(d) The speed corresponding to the maximum power output; and
(e) The maximum continuous operating speed.

In addition to the steady-state conditions, the engine shall be subjected to transient speed changes, including rapid acceleration and deceleration, across the full operating range. These transient tests are intended to identify any resonant conditions that may not be apparent during steady-state operation. The vibration measurements shall be taken at the engine mounting points and at critical structural components, in accordance with the methods specified in the applicable AMC. The results shall demonstrate that the vibration levels do not exceed the limits established in CS-E.10.

The calibration curves for the engine shall be established as a function of the following parameters:

(a) Engine power output, expressed in kilowatts (kW) or horsepower (hp);
(b) Engine speed, expressed in revolutions per minute (r/min);
(c) Fuel flow rate, expressed in kilograms per hour (kg/h) or pounds per hour (lb/h);
(d) Fuel pressure, expressed in kilopascals (kPa) or pounds per square inch (psi);
(e) Oil pressure, expressed in kilopascals (kPa) or pounds per square inch (psi);
(f) Oil temperature, expressed in degrees Celsius (°C) or degrees Fahrenheit (°F);
(g) Cylinder head temperature, expressed in degrees Celsius (°C) or degrees Fahrenheit (°F);
(h) Intake air temperature, expressed in degrees Celsius (°C) or degrees Fahrenheit (°F);
(i) Ambient pressure, expressed in kilopascals (kPa) or inches of mercury (inHg);
(j) Mixture ratio, expressed as a percentage of stoichiometric mixture or as a fuel-to-air ratio;
(k) Ignition timing, expressed in degrees before top dead center (BTDC);
(l) Propeller pitch, if applicable, expressed in degrees or as a ratio;
(m) Engine vibration levels, expressed in inches per second (in/s) or millimeters per second (mm/s);
(n) Exhaust gas temperature, expressed in degrees Celsius (°C) or degrees Fahrenheit (°F);
(o) Boost pressure, if applicable, expressed in kilopascals (kPa) or pounds per square inch (psi);
(p) Fuel temperature, expressed in degrees Celsius (°C) or degrees Fahrenheit (°F).

These parameters shall be measured under steady-state conditions at various points across the engine's operating envelope, including idle, cruise, and maximum continuous power settings.

The calibration curves for the engine shall be established as a function of the following parameters:

(a) Engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, as a function of engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, and ambient static pressure and temperature;

(b) Fuel flow, as a function of engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, and ambient static pressure and temperature;

(c) Turbine inlet temperature (TIT) or turbine exhaust temperature (TET), as a function of engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, and ambient static pressure and temperature;

(d) Engine rotational speed, as a function of engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, and ambient static pressure and temperature;

(e) Compressor discharge pressure, as a function of engine pressure ratio (EPR) or net thrust, and ambient static pressure and temperature;

(f) Fuel flow, as a function of engine rotational speed and ambient static pressure and temperature, for idle and minimum stable operation conditions.

The calibration curves shall be derived from test data obtained in accordance with CS-E.10. The data shall be corrected to standard atmosphere conditions where applicable, and the uncertainty of the measurements shall be within the limits specified in CS-E.10. The calibration curves shall be used to verify engine performance and to determine the engine operating limits.

