For a turbine engine installed in a multi-engined aeroplane, the following additional power ratings may be established, in addition to the Take-off and Maximum Continuous ratings:

(a) A Take-off rating, which is the maximum power or thrust available for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for take-off;

(b) A Climb rating, which is the maximum power or thrust available for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for climb;

(c) A Maximum Continuous rating, which is the maximum power or thrust available for continuous operation;

(d) A Maximum Continuous rating for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for use in the event of an engine failure;

(e) A Maximum Continuous rating for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for use in the event of an engine failure, where the engine is required to operate at a higher power or thrust level than the Maximum Continuous rating for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for use in the event of an engine failure;

(f) A Maximum Continuous rating for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for use in the event of an engine failure, where the engine is required to operate at a higher power or thrust level than the Maximum Continuous rating for a specified time, not exceeding 5 minutes, for use in the event of an engine failure.

The continued rotation of the main rotating systems following an in-flight shutdown shall not result in damage to the engine or airframe, or in a hazardous condition for the aircraft. The assessment of this continued rotation shall consider the following conditions:

a) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified takeoff power setting;
b) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified continuous power setting;
c) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified takeoff power setting with the aircraft at sea level, standard atmosphere, and zero calibrated airspeed;
d) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified continuous power setting with the aircraft at the maximum certified operating altitude, standard atmosphere, and zero calibrated airspeed;
e) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified takeoff power setting with the aircraft at the maximum certified operating altitude, standard atmosphere, and zero calibrated airspeed;
f) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified continuous power setting with the aircraft at sea level, standard atmosphere, and zero calibrated airspeed;
g) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified takeoff power setting with the aircraft at the maximum certified operating altitude, standard atmosphere, and maximum calibrated airspeed;
h) The engine is shut down at the maximum certified continuous power setting with the aircraft at sea level, standard atmosphere, and maximum calibrated airspeed.

The assessment shall include the effects of any residual thrust or drag, and the potential for fire or fume generation.
